# Day 5 - DHCP and DNS

## DHCP

DHCP stands for Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol.

DHCP automatically provides devices with network configuration such as:
- IP address
- Subnet mask
- Default gateway
- DNS server

This makes network configuration easier because users do not need to manually configure an IP address.

## DNS

DNS stands for Domain Name System.

DNS translates domain names into IP addresses.

Example:

google.com → IP address

This allows users to access websites using easy-to-remember domain names instead of numerical IP addresses.

## Practical Learning

I used the following commands in Windows Command Prompt:

### 1. ipconfig /all

This command displays detailed network configuration, including:
- IPv4 address
- DHCP status
- DHCP server
- DNS servers
- Default gateway

### 2. nslookup google.com

This command was used to check DNS resolution and observe the DNS server and IP address returned for google.com.

## Key Takeaway

DHCP helps devices obtain network configuration automatically, while DNS translates domain names into IP addresses. 
Both are important concepts in networking and cybersecurity.
